llama.cpp b11379 allocates a context's compute buffers for a reserve graph built
from a ubatch without decision spans. A clef graph's head inputs grow with the
spans of the real batch, so the first pass of a context sized to its prompt
reallocates them, and the driver holds the old buffers until it frees them: a
16k-token Clef pass peaked at 14.1 GiB of VRAM instead of 10.1 GiB. For the clef
arch the reserve only plans the splits; the first pass allocates its buffers
once, for its real graph. Upstream: not filed yet.

